kenyans are struggling with rising food  and fuel prices but no expenses are  being spared in election campaigns  deputy president william ruto is one of  two main contenders  political alliances shifted dramatically  after he fell out with his boss outgoing  president uhuru kenyatta  ruto is among the candidates talking  about the cost of living but they're not  always explaining exactly how they'll  address it  we will organize the economy and reduce  the cost of living  his main opponent raila rodinga has run  for president four times before  many believe he's been rigged out of  some past election victories  this time the historically dominant  political establishment is backing him  up  [Music]  corruption is our number one enemy  kenyan's money goes into individuals  pockets outgoing president uhuru  kenyatta is among those backing raila  activists say kenyatta's government has  been even more corrupt than those that  came before it  ryla's promising change  ruto who's held the second most powerful  office in kenya for the last nine years  is also promising change  many kenyans are not convinced by  promises to tackle corruption it's a  card that they play sort of  i try and excite the public and get  voted but once they're in power we have  seen what they do when they have power  and none of that convinces me that they  have any will or any desire to fight  corruption  some previous elections were contested  largely along ethnic lines  this time the politicians are talking  more about policy especially compared to  the campaigns in 2007  those polls were followed by ethnically  targeted violence more than a thousand  people were killed you now have the main  tribes all  represented in the coalition so because  every vote counts  um  the parties do not have the luxury of  demonizing  any particular area  opinion polls put ruto slightly behind  ryla but with enough voters still  undecided to swing it  a significant amount of money is  expected to change hands in the final  stages of the campaigns kenyans are due  to vote in two weeks time  malcolm webb al jazeera kitangela kenya
 

